Part I — Foundations

Chapter 1. Introduction

1.1 The problem

A citizen submits a request for information to a public institution. The institution is legally obligated to respond within a statutory deadline. The relevant information is dispersed across departmental silos that are only partially interoperable. Some information is subject to statutory exemptions. The response must be legally compliant, evidence-grounded, and complete.

The naive formulation treats this as a function

f: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{R}

and asks for a system \hat{f} that minimizes \mathbb{E}[d(f(q), \hat{f}(q))] for some distance d on responses.

This formulation is theoretically sterile for three reasons.

First, it fuses distinct failure modes into a single scalar. A response that fails to find information, a response that finds information but misrepresents it, and a response that misapplies the law are all "errors" under d. They are not the same error.

Second, it treats legal compliance as a soft penalty. A single unauthorized disclosure is not a small distance from a correct response. It is a violation of a hard constraint.

Third, it presupposes that f is well-defined. In the distributed setting, f depends on evidence that no single agent observes. The function f is an idealization, not an object.

1.2 The reframing

We replace f by the factorized composition

q \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{query}}} N(q) \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{route}}} C(q) \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{ret}}} \mathcal{E}(q) \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{evid}}} \Pi(q) \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{legal}}} \Lambda(q) \xrightarrow{F_{\mathrm{dec}}} r(q)

Each arrow has its own failure mode. Each failure mode has its own certification condition.

The architecture I would now lock is:

\boxed{
\textbf{Observability}
\rightarrow
\textbf{Identifiability}
\rightarrow
\textbf{Inference}
\rightarrow
\textbf{Certification}
}

with distributed retrieval sitting underneath observability:

\mathcal D_1,\ldots,\mathcal D_K
\rightarrow
\mathcal E
\rightarrow
\Pi
\rightarrow
\Lambda
\rightarrow
r.

1.3 The central question

Under what conditions can an LLM-mediated system produce a legally compliant information response when evidence and statutory constraints are distributed across partially isolated domains — and can this compliance be certified without trusting the generator?

