Chapter 2. The Factorization

2.1 Intermediate spaces

Definition 2.1.1. The information-need space \mathcal{N} consists of atomic, verifiable requests for a single piece of information. A request q induces a finite set N(q) \subseteq \mathcal{N} via F_{\mathrm{query}}.

Definition 2.1.2. The coalition space is 2^{[K]}, where [K] = \{1, \ldots, K\} indexes departmental silos.

Definition 2.1.3. The evidence space is 2^{\mathcal{D}}.

Definition 2.1.4. The proposition space \Pi consists of factual propositions expressible over the domain of \mathcal{D}.

Definition 2.1.5. The legal conclusion space \Lambda consists of legal conclusions expressible over the deontic base \mathcal{K}.

Definition 2.1.6. The demand-direction space \mathcal{A} consists of orthogonal dimensions along which evidence can vary. A retrieved set S \subseteq \mathcal{D} is demand-covering if its projection onto each a \in \mathcal{A} spans the range required by q.

2.2 The arrows

F_{\mathrm{query}}: \mathcal{Q} \to 2^{\mathcal{N}}

decomposes a request into atomic needs.

F_{\mathrm{route}}: 2^{\mathcal{N}} \to 2^{2^{[K]}}

assigns to each need a set of admissible coalitions.

F_{\mathrm{ret}}: 2^{\mathcal{N}} \times 2^{2^{[K]}} \to 2^{\mathcal{D}}

retrieves documents from the selected silos.

F_{\mathrm{evid}}: 2^{\mathcal{D}} \to 2^{\Pi}

extracts supported propositions from retrieved documents.

F_{\mathrm{legal}}: 2^{\Pi} \times \mathcal{K} \to 2^{\Lambda}

derives legal conclusions.

F_{\mathrm{dec}}: 2^{\Lambda} \to \mathcal{R}

produces the response.

2.3 Non-factorizability of error

Proposition 2.3.1 (NP). There is no single scalar metric d on \mathcal{R} such that the failure modes of F_{\mathrm{ret}}, F_{\mathrm{evid}}, and F_{\mathrm{legal}} are simultaneously and injectively represented.

Proof. Consider two responses r_1, r_2 \in \mathcal{R} with identical surface form but different provenance: r_1 derives from correctly retrieved and correctly interpreted evidence, r_2 from incorrectly retrieved evidence that happens to support the same surface claim. Any d depending only on (r_1, r_2) assigns d(r_1, r_2) = 0. Hence d cannot distinguish a failure of F_{\mathrm{ret}} from a success of F_{\mathrm{ret}}. \square

The factorization is therefore not cosmetic. It is necessary for the theory to have content.

