Part II — Coverage

Chapter 3. Coverage as a Composite Property

3.1 Definition

Definition 3.1.1. For a request q with needs N(q) = \{n_1, \ldots, n_m\}, define indicator variables

Z_i = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if need } n_i \text{ is correctly covered} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

The coverage of q is

\mathrm{Cov}(q) = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} Z_i

3.2 Coverage is composite

Observation 3.2.1. Z_i = 1 requires the conjunction of three conditions:

1. n_i \in N(q) (decomposition)
2. Some k with \mathrm{Rel}(n_i, \mathcal{D}_k) \neq \emptyset is in C(n_i) (routing)
3. Some relevant d_j \in \mathcal{D}_k is in R_k(n_i) (retrieval)

Write Z_i = D_i \cdot R_i \cdot T_i.

Consequence. Coverage failure can arise at any of three levels.

