Chapter 4. Demand-Direction Coverage Bound

4.1 The limitation of the union bound

The union bound in earlier drafts assumed that coverage failure is a sum of per-need retrieval failures. This model is correct but incomplete. Recent work on information-demand coverage shows that the binding constraint is often selection-time discard: the evidence is already in the candidate pool, and the bottleneck is set selection along demand dimensions. Retrieval recall is high; the failure is in spanning the demand directions required by the request.

GeoRAG formalizes demand-dimension coverage rate as the fraction of queries for which at least one selected chunk supports a given demand peak. The key finding is that both-peaks coverage rises dramatically under demand-direction optimization, and this coverage gap tracks downstream performance.

4.2 Hypotheses

(H-cov-1) F_{\mathrm{query}} is complete: every need expressed in q corresponds to some n_i \in N(q). (EA)

(H-cov-2) F_{\mathrm{route}} is surjective onto relevant modules: for each n_i, there exists k with \mathrm{Rel}(n_i, \mathcal{D}_k) \neq \emptyset and k \in C(n_i). (EA)

(H-cov-3) Retrieval is \varepsilon-complete: for every pair (n_i, k) with a relevant d_j \in \mathcal{D}_k,

P(d_j \in R_k(n_i)) \geq 1 - \varepsilon_{i,k}

(H-cov-4) The demand-direction projection of \mathcal{D} is \delta-covering: for each a \in \mathcal{A}, the probability that a random document set of size |S| spans the required range is at least 1 - \delta_a.

4.3 Proposition

Proposition 4.3.1 (Demand-direction coverage bound — NP). Under (H-cov-1)–(H-cov-4), the probability of coverage failure satisfies

P(\text{coverage failure}) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a

Proof. Define A_i = \{n_i \text{ not covered by retrieval}\}. By (H-cov-3), P(A_i) \leq \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k}. Define B_a = \{\text{selected evidence fails to span required range along } a\}. By (H-cov-4), P(B_a) \leq \delta_a. Coverage fails if \bigcup_i A_i \cup \bigcup_a B_a. By the union bound:

P\left(\bigcup_i A_i \cup \bigcup_a B_a\right) \leq \sum_i P(A_i) + \sum_a P(B_a) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a

This is a legitimate union bound. \square

Remark. The exponential term \exp(-2|S|^2/|\mathcal{D}|) in earlier drafts has been removed. It lacked a defined random variable, expectation, deviation inequality, and sampling model. A finite-population concentration bound requires a deviation parameter and a specified sampling mechanism. Such a theorem could be added later if the following are specified: the sampling mechanism, the finite population, the demand-direction variable, the expected coverage, the deviation threshold, and the dependence structure.

Corollary 4.3.2. For fixed per-need failure \varepsilon and demand-direction failure \delta, coverage is guaranteed at level 1 - \gamma only if

m \varepsilon + |\mathcal{A}| \delta \leq \gamma

Requests with many needs require proportionally better per-need retrieval and demand-direction coverage.

