Chapter 5. Coverage Lower Bound

5.1 The conjecture

Conjecture 5.1.1 (CO). There exists a family of request distributions \{\mu_\theta\} and silo partitions \{\mathcal{D}^{(K)}\} such that, for every generator G and every routing policy \pi,

P(\text{coverage failure}) \geq c(\theta, K) > 0

where c is bounded below independently of |G| and of the per-silo retrieval quality.

Falsification condition. Exhibit a family of request distributions and silo partitions for which coverage failure can be driven to zero by increasing generator capacity or retrieval quality alone.

5.2 Partial result: disjoint identifier spaces

Proposition 5.2.1 (NP — corrected). Let \mathcal{D}^{(K)} be a partition of \mathcal{D} into K silos such that no two silos share a common identifier space, and let \mu_\theta be a request distribution whose needs are uniformly distributed across the K silos with no cross-silo index. Then for any routing policy \pi that queries at most c < K silos:

For one need n_i:

P(Z_i = 1) \leq \frac{c}{K}

Therefore:

\boxed{
P(Z_i = 0) \geq 1 - \frac{c}{K}
}

If silo assignments are independent across m needs:

P(\mathrm{Cov}(q) = 1) \leq \left(\frac{c}{K}\right)^m

Proof. Each need n_i is relevant to exactly one silo k_i. A routing policy that queries c silos covers need n_i only if k_i \in C. Since the k_i are uniformly distributed over [K], P(Z_i = 1) = c/K. The lower bound P(Z_i = 0) \geq 1 - c/K follows immediately. Under independence, P(\text{all }m\text{ needs covered}) = (c/K)^m. \square

Note. The earlier claim "the probability that all needs are covered is at most c/K" was incorrect. The correct expression under independence is (c/K)^m. The one-need result P(Z_i = 1) \leq c/K is sufficient for the lower bound.

This establishes Conjecture 5.1.1 for the restricted family of disjoint-identifier silo partitions. The general case remains open.

