Part III — Evidence Identifiability

Chapter 6. Level Separation

6.1 Four levels

\boxed{d \;\to\; e \;\to\; p \;\to\; \ell}

· d \in \mathcal{D}: document
· e \in \mathcal{E}: evidence (a document with provenance)
· p \in \Pi: factual proposition
· \ell \in \Lambda: legal conclusion

6.2 Proposition

Proposition 6.2.1 (NP). In general,

d \models p \;\;\not\Rightarrow\;\; d \models \ell

Proof. By construction. Take \mathcal{K} to be any deontic base not containing the rule that converts p to \ell. Then d can support p without supporting \ell. \square

6.3 The role of \mathcal{K}

The transition from evidence to legal conclusion passes through \mathcal{K}:

(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{E}) \vdash \ell

It is not factorizable through a single material implication.

6.4 Operational realization

The neuro-symbolic legal reasoning literature has operationalized exactly this separation. Governatori and Palmirani present a tool that provides explanations for legal decisions based on provisions encoded in LegalRuleML, using Defeasible Deontic Logic (DDL) to reason about legal provisions and generate explanations. The DDL explanation tool is implemented in Python, using the CLINGO Python API to integrate the explanation module with the implementation of DDL in ASP.

A verifier that checks only factual support checks only half the chain. Sound certification requires both.

