Chapter 7. Signature Impossibility

7.1 Observable σ-algebra

Definition 7.1.1. For a need n and retrieval R(n), the signature observables are

\mathcal{O}(n) = \sigma\!\left(n,\; R(n),\; \{\mathrm{sim}(n,d)\}_{d \in R(n)},\; \{\mathrm{meta}(d)\}_{d \in R(n)}\right)

7.2 Proposition

Proposition 7.2.1 (NP — Signature impossibility). Suppose there exist d, d' \in \mathcal{D} and a proposition p such that:

· \mathrm{sim}(n, d) = \mathrm{sim}(n, d')
· \mathrm{meta}(d) = \mathrm{meta}(d')
· d \models p and d' \not\models p

Then no \mathcal{O}(n)-measurable verifier V can certify p.

Proof. V is a function of \mathcal{O}(n). The two scenarios produce identical inputs for V. The correct outputs differ. Contradiction. \square

7.3 Escape principles

The literature identifies three architectural principles that escape the text-only impossibility:

1. State Exteriority: access to world state distinguishing answerable from unanswerable queries.
2. Verification Independence: training signal decoupled from task performance.
3. Provenance Binding: creating a verification tier for externally checkable failure modes.

These are necessary conditions, not sufficient.

Strengthened requirement. Cross-provider verification — fanning one claim across multiple LLMs — separates none of them from noise. Verification independence must be structural, requiring different representational substrate, not merely different model weights.

