Part IV — Legal Constraint Satisfaction

Chapter 9. Defeasible Deontic Base

9.1 Definition

Definition 9.1.1. A defeasible deontic base is a tuple \mathcal{K} = (\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{R}, \succ, \models) where:

· \mathcal{O} is a legal ontology defining concepts, roles, and relations.
· \mathcal{R} is a set of defeasible deontic rules of the form
  \varphi \Rightarrow \mathcal{O}p(\psi), \quad \varphi \Rightarrow \mathcal{P}erm(\psi), \quad \varphi \Rightarrow \mathcal{F}orb(\psi)
  with explicit defeaters and exclusion guards.
· \succ is a priority relation on rules.
· \models is a satisfaction relation over possible worlds.

9.2 Decidability for a restricted fragment

Proposition 9.2.1 (ER — Governatori et al.). For the fragment \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{poly}} of defeasible deontic logic without conjunctive obligations, the extension of a defeasible theory can be computed in linear time in the size of the theory.

Attribution. By the results of Governatori et al. (2023), the proof conditions for defeasible provability yield a bottom-up construction that computes the extension in O(n \cdot r) time. The algorithm Evaluate Conjunctive Obligation extends this to conjunctive obligations in polynomial time. Propositional Well-Founded Defeasible Logic theories can be computed in polynomial time.

Corollary 9.2.2. For \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{poly}}, the relation \vdash_{\mathrm{syn}} is decidable in polynomial time.

9.3 The syntactic / interpretive gap

Observation 9.3.1. Under natural inclusion, \vdash_{\mathrm{syn}} \subseteq \vdash_{\mathrm{int}}.

Observation 9.3.2. There exist cases where \mathcal{K} \vdash_{\mathrm{int}} \varphi but \mathcal{K} \not\vdash_{\mathrm{syn}} \varphi.

These cases arise from: contextual definitions, exceptions to exceptions, balancing tests, temporality, jurisdictional scope, interpretive authority, conflicts between provisions, vague standards.

9.4 Parameterizing the gap

Definition 9.4.1. A fragment hierarchy \{\mathcal{K}_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A}} is a family of defeasible deontic bases such that:

1. \mathcal{K}_{\alpha} \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\beta} for \alpha \preceq \beta.
2. \vdash_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\alpha} = \vdash_{\mathrm{int}}^{\alpha} is decidable for each \alpha.
3. The interpretive gap G_\alpha = \vdash_{\mathrm{int}}^{\alpha} \setminus \vdash_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\alpha} is characterized by the priority resolution required at level \alpha.

