Chapter 10. Hard Constraints Versus Loss

10.1 The distinction

Let D(r) indicate whether r contains an unauthorized disclosure.

Definition 10.1.1. A constraint is hard if its violation is not compensable by improvement in any other dimension.

Proposition 10.1.1 (NP). Unauthorized disclosure is a hard constraint.

Proof. By the structure of statutory non-disclosure: a single unauthorized disclosure cannot be remedied by producing additional correct information. It is a categorical violation. \square

10.2 Consequence for optimization

The correct optimization is

\max_\pi \mathbb{E}[U(\pi, q)] \quad \text{subject to} \quad P(D = 1) \leq \varepsilon_D

with \varepsilon_D = 0 in practice. This is not the same as adding a penalty term.

10.3 Disclosure firewall

Architecture 10.3.1. Place a deterministic PII/exemption filter after F_{\mathrm{legal}} and before F_{\mathrm{dec}}, operating on extracted propositions rather than generated text.

Proposition 10.3.2 (NP — conditional). If the firewall is deterministic, operates on the proposition set \Pi(q), and \Pi(q) is produced by a trusted extraction process, then its correctness is independent of the generator G.

Proof. The firewall is a function \mathrm{FW}: \Pi \to \{\text{pass}, \text{block}\} that depends only on the propositions and the exemption rules in \mathcal{K}. If \Pi(q) is trusted, the firewall's correctness is a property of the proposition set and the rule base, not of the generator. \square

Note. This is conditional. If \Pi(q) is produced by a generator-dependent extractor, then the firewall's end-to-end correctness depends on G.

