Part V — Distributed Retrieval

Chapter 11. Federated Retrieval

11.1 The decision problem

Given a need n, the federated retrieval problem is to select a query transformation Q_k for each silo k such that the union of returned documents spans the demand directions of n.

11.2 Complexity

Observation 11.2.1. Under submodularity of the coverage function, greedy approximation achieves (1 - 1/e)-approximation.

Assumption 11.2.2 (EA). The coverage function \mathrm{Cov}_{\mathcal{A}} is submodular in the query transformation.

11.3 Federated representation exchange

Proposition 11.3.1 (NP — corrected). Federated RAG architectures that exchange compact latent representations rather than raw documents can enable cross-silo retrieval while reducing raw-document exposure.

Attribution. FedRepRAG keeps raw documents at their owning clients and exchanges only compact latent representations during cross-client retrieval. However, FedRepRAG "reduces direct raw-content exposure but does not guarantee confidentiality of exchanged representations or formal end-to-end privacy. Our analysis quantifies residual representation-level leakage".

Corrected statement: The architecture can reduce raw-document exposure while enabling cross-silo retrieval. Confidentiality is a measurable leakage property:

L_{\mathrm{repr}} = I(X;\,Z)

where X is the raw document and Z is the exchanged representation.

