Chapter 12. Sources Versus Origins

12.1 Dependency graph

Definition 12.1.1. The information dependency graph G_E = (V_E, E_E) has as vertices the cited sources, and as edges s_i \to s_j whenever s_j depends informationally on s_i.

12.2 Independent origins

Definition 12.2.1. Define the equivalence relation:

s_i \sim s_j \iff s_i, s_j \text{ have the same informational origin}

Let V_0 = \{v \in V_E : \deg_{\mathrm{in}}(v) = 0\}. Then:

\boxed{
\mathrm{Ind}(G_E) = |V_0 / \sim|
}

12.3 Proposition

Proposition 12.3.1 (NP). In general,

|\text{citations}| \neq |\text{independent origins}|

Proof. Take N sources all copying the same primary base. Then |V_E| = N but \mathrm{Ind}(G_E) = 1. \square

12.4 Algorithm

Algorithm 12.4.1 (Independent-origin count).

1. Construct the dependency graph G_E.
2. Compute the condensation G_E^{\mathrm{SCC}} by contracting strongly connected components.
3. Identify vertices with in-degree zero in G_E^{\mathrm{SCC}}.
4. Quotient by informational redundancy \sim.
5. Return the cardinality of the quotient.

Proposition 12.4.2 (NP — conditional). Algorithm 12.4.1 correctly computes \mathrm{Ind}(G_E) in O(|V_E| + |E_E|) time conditional on the equivalence relation \sim already being available or computable in linear time.

Proof. The condensation and in-degree computation are linear in the size of the graph. The quotient by \sim is linear in the number of zero-in-degree vertices, given \sim. \square

Note. If \sim is based on semantic-parse equivalence, computing semantic equivalence may not be linear. The complexity claim is conditional.

12.5 Consequence

Triangulation must measure independent origins, not citation counts. Any claimed "triangulation" score must report both citation count and independent-origin count.

