Part VI — Certification

Chapter 13. The Target Theorem

13.1 Setup

A certification architecture is a tuple (\mathrm{Ext}, V) with:

· \mathrm{Ext}: \mathcal{D} \to \Pi, an evidence extractor
· V: \Pi \times \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{R} \to \{0, 1\}, a verifier

Independence requirement. \mathrm{Ext} is independent of G. It is not obtained by prompting G, nor by distillation from G.

13.2 Sub-hypotheses

(H4a) Extraction soundness. For the target class of documents and propositions, define the extraction risk functional:

R_E = P_{(d,p)\sim\mu}[\mathrm{Ext}(d)\text{ incorrectly asserts }p]

Then assume:

\boxed{
R_E \leq \epsilon_E
}

This replaces the earlier use of precision \pi_E. Precision is \frac{TP}{TP+FP}, which does not automatically imply P(\text{extraction failure}) \leq 1 - \pi_E. The theorem needs a formally defined risk.

(H4b) Trusted-component independence. The verifier's implementation, parameters, calibration data, and decision procedure are not derived from or controlled by the generator.

\boxed{
\mathrm{State}(V) \cap \mathrm{State}(G) = \varnothing
}

except for explicitly specified public inputs. This replaces the earlier conditional mutual information statement I(V(\Pi,\mathcal K,r);G \mid \Pi,\mathcal K,r)=0, which was trivially true for any deterministic V and did not establish architectural independence.

(H4c) Compositional risk control. The composed risk bound on undetected incorrect claims is:

\boxed{
R_{\mathrm{cert}} \leq \epsilon
}

for a specified risk functional. Conformal methods may establish this for particular classes, but the theorem is framework-neutral.

13.3 Statement

Theorem 13.3.1 (Target, conditional — NP). Under (H-cov-1)–(H-cov-4), (H4a), (H4b), and (H4c):

P(\text{sound certification}) \geq 1 - \varepsilon_C - \varepsilon_E - \varepsilon_L - \varepsilon_{\mathrm{crypto}}

where:

· \varepsilon_C = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a is coverage failure probability (Proposition 4.3.1).
· \varepsilon_E is evidence-extraction error (H4a).
· \varepsilon_L is legal-inference error (Proposition 9.2.1).
· \varepsilon_{\mathrm{crypto}} is cryptographic failure probability.

Proof. By (H-cov), coverage holds with probability \geq 1 - \varepsilon_C. Conditioned on coverage, evidence extraction succeeds with probability \geq 1 - \varepsilon_E by (H4a). Conditioned on extraction, legal inference succeeds with probability \geq 1 - \varepsilon_L by (H5). The cryptographic commitment is sound except with probability \varepsilon_{\mathrm{crypto}}. Union bound on the complements gives the result. \square

13.4 The named gap

H4a is the non-trivial hypothesis. The verifier must distinguish the cases of Proposition 7.2.1, which requires access to propositional content and to an extractor independent of the generator.

13.5 What the theorem achieves

The bound is additive. Each error source is treated separately. Fusing errors into a single metric would prevent this decomposition.

