Chapter 14. Cryptographic Certification

14.1 Two certificate architectures

Public certificate.

C_{\mathrm{pub}} = H(q, N, C, \mathcal{E}, \Pi, \Lambda, r) \oplus \sigma_{\mathrm{Ext}} \oplus \sigma_V \oplus \tau

provides integrity and binding: the response is bound to the evidence and propositions.

Zero-knowledge certificate.

\pi_{\mathrm{ZK}} = \mathrm{Prove}(w, x) \quad \text{such that} \quad \mathrm{Verify}(x, \pi_{\mathrm{ZK}}) = 1

without revealing witness w, provides confidentiality.

The distinction.

\boxed{
\text{Integrity/binding}
\neq
\text{confidentiality}
\neq
\text{zero-knowledge}
}

14.2 Binding properties

Proposition 14.2.1 (NP — corrected). Under standard cryptographic assumptions:

1. Binding: the response r is bound to \mathcal{E}(q), \Pi(q), and \Lambda(q).
2. Temporal ordering: the timestamp \tau establishes order.
3. Audit correctness: any auditor can verify without access to G.

Proof. Binding follows from the hash chain. Temporal ordering follows from the trusted timestamp. Audit correctness follows from the soundness of V and \mathrm{Ext}. \square

14.3 Zero-knowledge compliance

Proposition 14.3.1 (NP — conditional on ZK primitives). Legal compliance predicates over distributed evidence can be expressed as zero-knowledge statements.

Attribution. A recent proposed architecture, ZKAP (Zero-Knowledge Audit Protocol), illustrates one possible implementation. ZKAP couples a machine-learning inference to a zero-knowledge proof of constraint satisfaction, with a Certified Stack cryptographically binding model weights, integrity policy, runtime stack, and hardware configuration under a single commitment. However, ZKAP is a proposed architecture, with implementation components explicitly described as under development.

The proposition is conditional on the cryptographic primitives actually satisfying the required properties. ZKAP exists as a proposed architecture does not imply ZKAP establishes the proposition.

