Chapter 15. The Cost of Distrust

15.1 The conjecture

Conjecture 15.1.1 (CO). Certification without trust imposes a coverage overhead growing with source distribution:

\varepsilon_1 - \varepsilon_0 = \Omega\left(\mathcal{H}(\mathcal{D})\right)

where \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{D}) is the silo-partition entropy.

Falsification condition. Exhibit a family of request distributions and silo partitions for which distrust imposes no coverage overhead.

15.2 What the partial result actually says

Proposition 15.2.1 (NP — corrected). Under a model in which no trusted precomputed semantic representation is available and each document must be independently parsed by \mathrm{Ext}:

\mathrm{Cost}(\mathrm{Ext}) \geq \sum_{d \in \mathcal{E}} \mathrm{Cost}(\mathrm{parse}(d))

Proof. Independence requires that \mathrm{Ext} does not use G's parameters or prompts. Under the stated model, it must parse each document from scratch. \square

Note. The earlier claim that "independence requires that Ext must parse each document from scratch" is false. An independent extractor could use cached representations, precomputed structured metadata, a trusted shared parser, batch processing, indexes, or hardware acceleration. Generator independence does not imply computational independence from all previous processing. The correct statement is conditional on the model assumption.

