Part VIII — Open Problems

Chapter 18. Programme

18.1 Open problems

1. Prove or refute Conjecture 5.1.1 under precise hypotheses. The partial result for disjoint identifier spaces is a starting point.
2. Prove or refute Conjecture 15.1.1 by exhibiting a silo structure with zero cost of distrust.
3. Formalize H4a and exhibit generator-independent verifiers for a concrete legal domain.
4. Characterize observable sufficiency for restricted classes of \mathcal{K}.
5. Characterize the gap \vdash_{\mathrm{int}} \setminus \vdash_{\mathrm{syn}} for specific legal domains.
6. Establish a coverage lower bound in terms of silo partition entropy.
7. Implement the certificate data structure and demonstrate end-to-end soundness.
8. Publish the error-taxonomy benchmark.

18.2 The central question, restated

Under what conditions can an LLM-mediated system produce a legally compliant information response when evidence and statutory constraints are distributed across partially isolated domains — and can this compliance be certified without trusting the generator?

18.3 The general abstraction

\text{observations} \;\to\; \text{latent structure} \;\to\; \text{decision}

The common problem. Observation does not guarantee identification of the latent mechanism.


Conclusion

This dissertation has stated a theory of certification for legally constrained information responses under distributed evidence.

The central conditional answer:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Certification is possible if:}\\
\text{(1) required information is observable,}\\
\text{(2) extraction is sufficiently sound,}\\
\text{(3) legal inference is sound for the target fragment,}\\
\text{(4) the verifier is outside the generator's trust domain,}\\
\text{(5) the certificate binds the entire evidence-to-decision chain.}
\end{aligned}
}

Cryptography is the binding layer, not the truth layer.

The final certification condition:

\boxed{
\mathrm{Cert}(r) = 1
}

only if

\begin{cases}
C=1\\
E=1\\
L=1\\
D=0\\
B=1
\end{cases}

where B means that the certificate cryptographically binds the relevant state.

The probabilistic statement:

\boxed{
P(\mathrm{invalid\ certified\ response})
\leq
\epsilon_C+\epsilon_E+\epsilon_L+\epsilon_B
}

provided each \epsilon is actually defined as a risk term.

The real theory:

\boxed{
\text{No certification without sufficient observability;}
}

\boxed{
\text{no legal certification without sound inference;}
}

\boxed{
\text{no auditable certification without binding the inference chain.}
}

The theory is not complete. Its central hypothesis (H4a) is unresolved for the general case, though partial results exist for restricted fragments. But the question it poses — what can be certified independently of the generator? — is well-posed, and the framework is a substantial step toward answering it.

The rest is engineering.

