B.1 Corrections to Chapter 4 (Coverage Bound)

Original claim:

P(\text{coverage failure}) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a + \exp\left(-\frac{2|S|^2}{|\mathcal{D}|}\right)

Problem: The exponential term lacked a defined random variable, expectation, deviation inequality, and sampling model. The exponent was dimensionally suspicious — finite-population concentration bounds normally depend on a deviation parameter t, not simply |S|^2/|\mathcal{D}|.

Correction: Removed the exponential term entirely. The legitimate union bound is:

P(\text{coverage failure}) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a

Condition for re-adding a concentration term: Specify (1) sampling mechanism, (2) finite population, (3) demand-direction variable, (4) expected coverage, (5) deviation threshold, (6) dependence structure.

B.2 Corrections to Chapter 5 (Coverage Lower Bound)

Original claim: "The probability that all needs are covered is at most c/K."

Problem: Only P(Z_i=1) = c/K for one need was established. For m needs with independent uniform silo assignments, P(\text{all covered}) = (c/K)^m, not c/K.

Correction:

P(Z_i=1) \leq \frac{c}{K} \implies P(Z_i=0) \geq 1-\frac{c}{K}

Under independence:

P(\mathrm{Cov}(q)=1) \leq \left(\frac{c}{K}\right)^m

The one-need result P(Z_i=0) \geq 1-c/K is the cleanest formulation and does not require the questionable "all needs" step.

