Appendix H. Final Certification Condition

H.1 Certification predicate

\boxed{
\mathrm{Cert}(r) = 1
}

if and only if:

\begin{cases}
C=1 & \text{(coverage holds)} \\
E=1 & \text{(evidence valid)} \\
L=1 & \text{(legal inference valid)} \\
D=0 & \text{(no disclosure violation)} \\
B=1 & \text{(certificate cryptographically binds state)}
\end{cases}

H.2 Probabilistic statement

\boxed{
P(\mathrm{invalid\ certified\ response})
\leq
\epsilon_C+\epsilon_E+\epsilon_L+\epsilon_B
}

provided each \epsilon is defined as a risk term:

· \epsilon_C = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \min_{k \in C(n_i)} \varepsilon_{i,k} + \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \delta_a (coverage)
· \epsilon_E = R_E (extraction risk functional)
· \epsilon_L (legal inference error)
· \epsilon_B = \epsilon_{\mathrm{crypto}} (cryptographic failure)

H.3 The three foundational statements

\boxed{
\text{No certification without sufficient observability.}
}

\boxed{
\text{No legal certification without sound inference.}
}

\boxed{
\text{No auditable certification without binding the inference chain.}
}

H.4 The central conditional answer

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Certification without trusting the generator is possible if:}\\
\text{(1) required information is observable,}\\
\text{(2) extraction is sufficiently sound,}\\
\text{(3) legal inference is sound for the target fragment,}\\
\text{(4) the verifier is outside the generator's trust domain,}\\
\text{(5) the certificate binds the entire evidence-to-decision chain.}
\end{aligned}
}

Cryptography is the binding layer, not the truth layer.

